  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Conserving the endangered western hoolock gibbon in community-managed forests of Northeast India</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The endangered western hoolock gibbon (Hoolock hoolock) is threatened by loss of closed-canopy forests, and illegal hunting. In the hill states of Northeast India, the closed-canopy forests that gibbons inhabit is largely under the ownership and management of communities. While there is local conservation interest to secure forests and gibbons, these local initiatives are in need of outside support, largely to address two main threats to gibbons. The first is changing land-use patterns and erosion of traditional practices of conserving village forests are resulting in loss of the species habitat, while the second is that gibbons are being directly impacted by illegal hunting. Project objectives include to: (1) implement conservation initiatives to reduce threats to the western hoolock gibbon in select community-managed forests of Nagaland and Meghalaya; (2) assess the potential for gibbon conservation in community-managed forests of Khasi Hills and Ri-Bhoi districts, Meghalaya; and, (3) spread awareness of gibbons as a flagship species for closed-canopy forests in Northeast India.</narrative>
  </description>
